  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Noticeably cooler into Memorial Day weekend with\ntemperatures challenging record low maximums on Saturday.\n\nMainly clear skies and light winds have allowed for temperatures\nto fall into the upper 30s across northern PA this morning as\nhigh pressure builds into the region. While temperatures likely\nwon't fall to much farther from their current values before \nsunrise, some patchy frost is possible in the coldest locations.\n\nModel data shows the anomalous high pressure channeling southward\ndown the east side of the Appalachians, setting up a classic \ncool wedge/cold air damming (CAD) pattern into Memorial Day \nweekend. We continue to reiterate a bearish outlook for daytime \ntemps Fri-Sun with record challenging low maxes on the table for\nSaturday (see climate section below). \n\n850 mb show a highly anomalous V-component (wind from the SSE) \nat 18Z Sat with Standardized Anomalies of +3-4 St deviations \nover Central and NW PA, while winds around 925 mb highlight\nnegative U anomalies of the same magnitude (from the ESE).\n\nThis will create an expansive llvl CAD regime with strong\nmoisture advection aloft, leading to steady light to moderate \nrain/drizzle. The moderately gusty east/southeast wind will \nmake it feel downright dreary/raw, unseasonably chilly and \npretty miserable for this time of year - especially for outdoor\nactivities Friday afternoon through Sunday morning. \n\nThe 2-3 day cool stretch will certainly be noticeable following\nan early summerlike surge, but won't have staying power with \ntemperatures forecast to moderate and trend above normal into \nthe middle of next week.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Widespread soaking/beneficial rain dampers \noutdoor plans and activities to start Memorial Day weekend \n\nMainly dry conditions will continue through the morning before\nrain gradually spreads northward this afternoon (beginning \nmainly after 19-20Z near and to the north of I-80, and possibly \nnot until near or shortly after midnight near the PA/NY border) \nThe rain will continue through the day on Saturday, with the \nsteadiest rain expected during the late morning/early afternoon \nas a 40 to 50 knot low-level jet moves over the region.\n\nExpect widespread 1-2\" rainfall totals which will be very \nbeneficial for D1-D2 drought areas in the far southern tier into\nthe LSV. Anomalous upslope east-southeasterly flow may result \nin locally higher rainfall amounts approaching 3 inches across\nportions of south-central PA. The precip pattern transitions\nfrom steady rain/drizzle to more of a showery/low chance thunder\nregime for Sunday as the best forcing moves away from\nPennsylvania. Another round of steady rain is possible Sunday\nnight into Monday morning as another disturbance approaches from\nthe west.",
